                <pb facs="1043" n="927" sameAs="frus:frus1923v01/pg927"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">422.11 G 93/1258: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in Ecuador</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Bading</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1923-02-01T17:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">February 1,
                                1923—5 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>3. Your No. 1, January 24, 4 p.m.</p>
                <p>Arbitral provision paragraph 27, Contract of June 14, 1897, between Government of
                    Ecuador and railway company, confirmed by Section 6, Article 2 of decree of
                    1908, applies to “controversies and disputes which arise between the two parties
                    to this contract.” Thus, by agreement of the parties, arbitration is substituted
                    for trial by ordinary courts of all controversies and disputes between them.
                    This agreement evidently applies to questions of jurisdiction as well as to
                    questions of substantive right. Therefore, a denial of the right to resort to
                    arbitration in the present case is a denial of justice, and the Department
                    considers that you are justified in presenting the request of the railway
                    company, formally to the Ecuadoran Government, and authorizes you to take it up
                    with the President of Ecuador, as you suggest. Telegraph result.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
